{
  "name": "Mirror Reveal",
  "created": "2026-09-10T21:26:06.650Z",
  "notes": "Port of `mirror_brush`: a brush that paints while the room is loud, and whatever it painted scrolls outward from the centre, mirrored. The previous port was one line under a mirror on a triangle LFO, which is a shape bouncing rather than a trail being laid down.\n\nA TRAIL IS PARTICLES WITH A RATE, not a spawner with a trigger. `particles.rate` is a continuous stream in shapes per second, carried across frames so it means the same at 60 FPS as at 200, and the audio band drives it -- so loud paints densely, quiet paints nothing, and the brush going down and up is the band crossing its squelch. That is what makes `threshold` a real knob here: it IS the squelch.\n\n`mirror` maps a screen position to `|pos - centre| * 2`, so the emitter works in a half-strip where source 0 is the middle and source 1 is both ends. Every distance doubles with it: the animation's `min_speed` of 0.15 strip/s is 0.3 in this space, and the knob's ends are its declared 0.01-2 doubled. The animation's own comment says it folds with `abs(pos - 0.5)`; this is that fold as a warp.\n\n`colors` cycling one entry per stroke becomes `colouring: random`, a draw held for the life of each particle. Consecutive strokes therefore differ, but not in the animation's fixed rotation.\n\n`react_speed` and `react_brightness` are toggles and do not port, for the reason `10-perlin-audio` gives -- both default false, so this takes the constant each selects and the reaction lands on density instead. `max_speed` is then the depth of a row that is not there. `bloom` is the animation's falloff either side of a painted SEGMENT and here it is a particle's whole half-extent, which is a different measurement of the same softness.",
